{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I’ll be taking care of you during this stay. Can you tell me what’s been going on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:16:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I’ve had nausea and vomiting again, and bad belly pain. I’ve been doing that G-tube venting like before, and it helped a little, but then I started vomiting again even after venting."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:18:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You’ve had this before with partial bowel obstructions. Did you pass any stool or gas recently?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:19:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I had a solid bowel movement with a Fleet enema yesterday—started solid, got a bit loose—but no gas. I’m still not feeling right."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:20:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. We’ve reviewed your CT scan from today. It shows your small bowel is dilated, and there’s contrast in the large bowel, which suggests a partial blockage or ileus, similar to what you’ve had before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:21:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not completely blocked? That’s good, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. It’s partial, which means things are still moving, just slowly. We’ll manage this without surgery for now. You’ll be on nothing by mouth—NPO—for a few days, and your G-tube will be left open to gravity to help drain fluid and reduce pressure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:24:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I hate being on NPO. But I guess it’s necessary. What about pain? It’s pretty bad."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:25:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re giving you IV morphine every six hours, starting at 6 mg. We’ll adjust it based on your pain. But we have to be careful—too many narcotics can slow your bowels even more."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:26:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I understand. I’ve been on MS Contin at home. Can we keep that going?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:28:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ve continued your MS Contin 30 mg twice daily. We’ll also keep your pregabalin for nerve pain. And for nausea, we’re using Phenergan since you’re allergic to Compazine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:29:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I really can’t take Zofran—it doesn’t help anyway."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:30:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Noted. We’ll stick with Phenergan. Your labs show your hemoglobin is low—11.2—so we think you have iron deficiency anemia, likely from malabsorption. We’ll give you IV iron—Ferrlecit—tomorrow to help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:31:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That makes sense. I’ve always had trouble absorbing nutrients. What about my Coumadin? I’m on it for my arm clots."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2181-11-15 20:33:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re continuing your warfarin at 7.5 mg daily. Your INR is therapeutic, so we’re good there. We’ll keep monitoring it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2181-11-16 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How are you feeling today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2181-11-16 08:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Still nauseous, but maybe a little less pain. My G-tube drained over 1200 cc yesterday. Is that normal?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2181-11-16 08:02:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, that’s expected with a partial obstruction. The good news is your output is a sign the tube is working. We’re seeing it trend down today—already under 1000 cc."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2181-11-16 08:03:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. Any chance I can start drinking something soon?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2181-11-16 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not yet. We need to see your bowels start moving more normally. You’re still having some distension and tenderness. We’ll repeat the CT if things don’t improve."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2181-11-17 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi. Your G-tube output is now down to about 600 cc. That’s a great sign. You passed a bowel movement with Miralax yesterday—your lower pain improved after that, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2181-11-17 09:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, the lower pain is gone. The upper belly pain is still there, but less sharp."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2181-11-17 09:02:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The GI team suggested gentle abdominal massage to help move things along. It might help relieve the remaining blockage. Would you be okay trying that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2181-11-17 09:03:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Sure, I’ll try anything. Does it hurt?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2181-11-17 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It shouldn’t. We’ll do it gently. The idea is to stimulate motion past the sluggish part of your bowel. You might pass gas or feel some cramping—that’s a good sign."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2181-11-17 10:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great job. After the massage, you passed gas, and your G-tube output increased. That means things are moving. Your pain has improved too, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2181-11-17 10:31:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, much better. I feel like a weight lifted."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2181-11-17 10:32:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s exactly what we wanted. We’re starting you on peripheral TPN today—total parenteral nutrition—because you’ve been NPO for three days. It’ll give you calories and nutrients through your IV."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2181-11-17 10:33:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "TPN? I’ve had that before. It’s okay, but I’d rather eat."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2181-11-17 10:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I know. But we need to be sure your bowel is ready. We’ll try a full liquid diet tomorrow if you keep improving."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2181-11-18 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. You made it through the night without nausea or vomiting. We’re going to try a full liquid diet today—broth, juice, pudding. We’ll start slow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2181-11-18 08:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, I’m so happy! Can I have apple juice?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2181-11-18 08:02:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, small amounts. We’ll monitor you closely. If you tolerate it, we’ll keep you on liquids and clamp your G-tube to see if it stays down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2181-11-18 12:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The TPN is infusing now. You’re doing well on liquids so far. No vomiting, and your belly is softer."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2181-11-18 12:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel good. Just tired."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2181-11-18 12:02:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s normal after a few days of fasting. We’re also adjusting your pain meds. We’re stopping the IV morphine and switching you to oral MS Contin 30 mg three times a day. Less IV narcotics help your bowels recover."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2181-11-18 12:03:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. I’ll take the pills. Can I still have liquid morphine if it flares up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2181-11-18 12:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, 2.5 ml every four hours as needed. But we’ll watch for sedation. Your pregabalin dose is also adjusted—75 mg in the morning and at bedtime, plus one at noon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2181-11-19 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve had two full days on liquids with no vomiting. Your G-tube has been clamped, and you’re not having pain. We think you’re ready to go home tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2181-11-19 09:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Really? That’s wonderful! What do I need to do at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2181-11-19 09:02:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Stay on full liquids for one week. Take Miralax once a day to keep your bowels moving. Continue your regular meds—misoprostol for your stomach, pilocarpine for dry eyes, and warfarin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2181-11-19 09:03:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the pain pills?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2181-11-19 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll go home with MS Contin 30 mg every 12 hours for five days, then taper. Use liquid morphine for breakthrough pain. Call us if you have severe pain, fever, or can’t keep liquids down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2181-11-19 09:06:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Any follow-up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2181-11-19 09:07:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes—see your primary doctor and Dr. ___ in one month. We’ll also check your INR and iron levels."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2181-11-20 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—you’re being discharged today. You’ve done really well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2181-11-20 08:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you so much. I feel so much better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2181-11-20 08:02:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Remember: full liquids for a week, Miralax daily, and your meds. Walk every day to keep things moving. Call if anything worsens."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2181-11-20 08:03:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. I’m so grateful for the care."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2181-11-20 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take care. We’re here if you need us."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2181-11-15 19:39:00",
    "admission_end": "2181-11-20 15:10:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient, a woman with a complex history including fibrosing mesenteritis, recurrent partial bowel obstructions, and chronic GI dysmotility, was admitted for recurrent nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain. Imaging confirmed a partial small bowel obstruction, managed conservatively with NPO status, G-tube to gravity, IV fluids, and pain control. G-tube output decreased from over 1200 cc/day to 500–600 cc/day. She received IV iron for iron deficiency anemia and was started on TPN on hospital day 3. Abdominal massage recommended by GI helped stimulate bowel motility, leading to passage of gas and significant pain improvement. She was transitioned to a full liquid diet on day 4, tolerated it well, and was discharged on day 5. Pain management was transitioned from IV morphine to oral MS Contin and liquid morphine for breakthrough pain. She remained ambulatory throughout and was discharged home in good condition with instructions to continue full liquids and Miralax for one week.",
    "problems": [
      "Unspecified intestinal obstruction",
      "Sclerosing mesenteritis",
      "Iron deficiency anemia, unspecified",
      "Sicca syndrome",
      "Chronic hepatitis C without mention of hepatic coma",
      "Irritable bowel syndrome",
      "Asthma, unspecified type, unspecified",
      "Malnutrition of moderate degree",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Paralytic ileus",
      "Unspecified acquired hypothyroidism",
      "Long-term (current) use of anticoagulants",
      "Personal history of venous thrombosis and embolism",
      "Gastrostomy status"
    ]
  }
}
